  "system_prompt": "You are extracting the structural outline of a guitar method book from its front matter.\n\nThe user will give you the verbatim text of the first ~20 pages of a book.\nIdentify the chapters or major sections of the book (whichever the book uses\n\u2014 some method books use \"Lesson\", \"Chapter\", \"Part\", \"Section\", \"Unit\").\n\nOutput a JSON object with this shape:\n\n  { \"chapters\": [\n      { \"n\": 1, \"title\": \"...\", \"start_page\": <int>, \"end_page\": <int> },\n      ...\n  ] }\n\nRules:\n- `n` is the 1-indexed chapter number you assign (independent of the book's\n  own numbering \u2014 book may start at 0 or use Roman numerals).\n- `title` is the chapter's title as it appears in the book.\n- `start_page` is the first page of the chapter; `end_page` is the last.\n- If the front matter doesn't list page numbers, infer from the chapter\n  headings you see \u2014 start_page is where the chapter HEADING appears, and\n  end_page is one less than the next chapter's start_page (or the book's\n  last page for the final chapter).\n- Skip the table of contents itself, the title page, the copyright page,\n  the author bio \u2014 they are not \"chapters\" in this sense.\n- If the book is short and not subdivided, output a single chapter covering\n  the entire content.",
  "user_prompt": "Total pages in the book: 378.\nFirst 20 pages follow. Identify chapters.\n\n<<<TEXT\nSNS\n\nTABLE OF CONTENTS\n\nVOLUME ONE: NAME THAT CHORD\n\nDISCLAIMER/WARNING/CAUTION!!\nWHAT I\u00a2 THIS BOOK?\n\nTT WOULD SEEM THAT SOME THINGS ARE MISSING...\n\nNAME THAT CHORD\n\nClose & Spread\nClose & Spread\nClose & Spread\n\n6 Voicing Types\n6 Voicing Types\n6 Voicing Types\n\n6 Voicing Types\n6 Voicing Types\n6 Voicing Types\n\n6 Voicing Types\n6 Voicing Types\n6 Voicing Types\n\n6 Cycles\n6 Cycles\n6 Cycles\n\n6 Cycles\n6 Cycles\n6 Cycles\n\n6 Cycles\n6 Cycles\n6 Cycles\n\n6 Cycles\n6 Cycles\n6 Cycles\n\nC Major\nC Melodic Minor\nC Harmonic Minor\n\nC Major\nC Melodic Minor\nC Harmonic Minor\n\nC Major\nC Melodic Minor\nC Harmonic Minor\n\nC Major\nC Melodic Minor\nC Harmonic Minor\n\n\nVOLUME TWO: DO NOT NAME THAT CHORD\n\n*3-PART 4THS: Close & Spread\n*3-PART 4TH?: Close & Spread\n*3-PART 4THS: Close & Spread\n\n* Includes alternate voice-leadings\n\n4-PART 4THS: 6 Voicing Types\n4-PART 4THS: 6 Voicing Types\n4-PART 4THS: 6 Voicing Types\n\nSPREAD CLUSTER: 6 Voicing Types\nSPREAD CLUSTER: 6 Voicing Types\nSPREAD CLUSTER: 6 Voicing Types\n\n6 Cycles\n6 Cycles\n6 Cycles\n\n6 Cycles\n6 Cycles\n6 Cycles\n\n6 Cycles\n6 Cycles\n6 Cycles\n\nC Major\nC Melodic Minor\nC Harmonic Minor\n\nC Major\nC Melodic Minor\nC Harmonic Minor\n\nC Major\nC Melodic Minor\nC Harmonic Minor\n\n\na\n\nON NN\n\nPlease be advised that the author and publisher take no responsibility whatsoever\nfor any (and all) accidental and inadvertent self-inflicted injuries that may occur from\nexploring the material in this book. (This is especially relevant in regard to the left hand\nfingers, wrist, forearm, shoulder, muscles, tendons, bones, cartilage, nerves, etc., et al.)\n\nAny guitarist who works with this book is going to be exposed to literally hun-\ndreds of chords that involve stretches and fingering configurations that will be totally\nnew and unfamiliar. Be particularly cautious with descending voice-leadings that move\ndown the fingerboard. With each new descending chord, the distance between the frets\ngradually increases.\n\nEach (and every) guitarist must continuously monitor the sensations they perceive\nin their left (fingering) hand. Error on the side of being overly careful. \u201cNo pain\u2014is the\nway to go.\u201d Perhaps it would be even better to put it this way: \u201cNot even the slightest hint\nof any discomfort\u2014is the way to go.\u201d\n\nThe problem lies with the fact that a large percentage of this material is going to be\nnew to pretty much every guitarist who begins to work with it. When guitarists get a\nchance to play a whole bunch of stuff that is not only new to them, but also has a uniquely\ndifferent and compelling sound to it, there is a tendency to want to overdo it. All these\nnew sounds can have an almost narcotic-like effect. The ear perks up; the mind comes into\nsharp focus; the emotions begin to soar; the imagination begins to project a future when\nyou'll be able to use all this great stuff to completely blow people away(!)...all at the\nexpense of your left hand which, at this point, starts to feel like it\u2019s just been run over by a\ntruck.\n\nYou just got caught up in \u201cseduction by novelty.\u201d You stopped monitoring the sen-\nsations in your left (fingering) hand. You did too much. You over-extended your physical\nlimits. You over-stretched. You had a nice ride, for sure. But now you'll have to pay the\nprice for days, weeks, possibly months. (Believe me, I\u2019m not exaggerating!) In some cases,\nthe damage could involve years of recovery time. Occasionally, it could cause damage\nbeyond recovery time. The word for that is \u201cpermanent.\u201d The phrase in sports terminol-\nogy is \u201ccareer-ending.\u201d\n\nThink about it \u2014Picture yourself with permanent damage\u2014Now what?\n\nQ: \u2014 Mr. Goodchord, are you trying to scare us?\nA: You got that right!\n\nYou must exercise your sensitivity, your intelligence, your caution and your good\njudgement. No one else can do this for you. Only you can. And when you do, there should\nbe no problem.\n\nYou have been WARNED! (Please take good care of your hands!) Some sugges-\ntions and tentative guidelines/ restrictions will follow a bit later.\n\n\n\u201cWHAT I\u00a2 THIS BOOK?\u201d\n\nThis is a guitar sourcebook. It was written with an eye to the future of the next\nseveral generations of guitarists. It aims at anticipating certain \u201cneeds\u201d shared by all gui-\ntarists; those who are now old, middle-aged, young and those who are as yet unborn. It\nconsists of material derived from principles that are surprisingly simple and straightfor-\nward, but that imply potentials that are completely mind-blowing.\n\nThe scope and range of the material in this book (and several more that will follow,\nsoon enough), could easily overwhelm any single guitarist who begins to work with it.\nThat\u2019s totally O.K. Be overwhelmed! (It\u2019s good for you!) Just to give you an idea of how\nmuch material there is, (in Volumes I & II combined) I'll relate this incident:\n\nI recently went to Anaheim, CA to do a presentation at the 1.A,J.E. (International\nAssociation of Jazz Educators) for Berklee College of Music (where I currently teach) re-\ngarding this material. I had prepared a home-recorded CD for demonstration purposes.\nIn order to save myself time with the recording, I did it as a guitar quartet as opposed to\nplaying all the 4-part chords at one time by myself. The CD contained 30 examples in C\nMajor, 30 examples in C Melodic Minor, and 30 examples in C Harmonic Minor. They\nincluded all five chord-families in all 6 cycles through what I call the \u201cshort form,\u201d (7\ndifferent chords resolving to the next inversion of the first chord) but using only 1 of 6\npossible voicing types. The 90 examples totaled about 18 minutes. It represented about\n4.8% of the whole thing! (Without even considering the triads and the 3-part 4ths!) We\u2019re\ntalking HUGE here!\n\nThere is no need for any single guitarist to learn all this. Frankly, I think that it\u2019s not\nonly impossible but, more importantly, unnecessary. (Hopefully, all of this will become\nobvious after working with the material for 6 months to 1 year.)\n\nThis is a book about voice-leading. It\u2019s a book about chords. It\u2019s a book about using\nsimple mathematical ways of coming up with all the possibilities within certain pre-de-\ntermined and limited (although still enormous) parameters. (That's correct... ALL!)\n\nIt\u2019s a book about a number of other things as well. Some of them are difficult (if not\nimpossible) to put into words...Some others are easily described:\n\n* Better knowledge of the fingerboard\n\n\u00a2 Better knowledge of Major, Melodic Minor, and Harmonic Minor scales\n(both melodically and harmonically)\n\n\u00a9 Better knowledge of counterpoint\n\n\u00a2 Better knowledge of using open strings with other, fretted notes\n\n\u00ab Increased ability to move 2, 3, 4 (and possibly 5) voices more or less independently\n\n\u00a2 Increased comping skills\n\n* Increased compositional skills\n\n\u00a2 Increased improvisational skills\n\n* Increased teaching skills\n\n\u00a9 Increased humility\n\nThis is also the ultimate do-it-yourself book. How so? READ ON!\n\nPON IN INN\n\noN\n\na a\n\non. PON NN\n\na a a aa\n\n\no~\n\n\u201cIT WOULD SEEM THAT SOME THINGS ARE MISSING...\u201d\n\nCorrect. Much is missing. In fact, almost everything that one would deem important is\nmissing! Let me list a few: .\n\nThines # issing in this book:\n\n\u00a2 Detailed instructions on what to do e Dynamics\n\u00a2 Fingerings * Articulations\n* Position markers \u00a2 Time signatures\n* Alternate fingerings \u00a2 Rhythm\n* Open string indicators \u00ae String sets\n\u00a2 Tempos \u00a2 Tablature\n* Written musical notation\n\u00a2 This type of guitar info: 3\n(in spite of that, check out this chord.\n(I don\u2019t even know what it\u2019s called!) r \u00e9bu\nIt\u2019s called \u201cthe despair chord!\u201d\n<P 7\nIt lives in the Harmonic Major Scale...\nSorry, I couldn't resist!)\n\nWhat is not in this book is as deliberately omitted as what is included. A few examples:\n\n* Written musical notation is not used because not all guitarists can read\n\n* Tablature is not used for the same reason, plus the fact that it\u2019s \u201ctoo fingering specific\u201d\nfor our purposes\n\n* Consequently, any guitarist (and all guitarists) who at least know where the notes\nare on the instrament can use this book ;\n\n* Pretty much all of the other \u201comissions\u201d occur so that you, the individual guitarist,\nhave the unimpeded, uninfluenced, unprecedented and (probably) unwanted\nopportunity to figure it all out by yourself! (Thus, the ultimate do-it-yourself book.)\n\nThe less that is explained, the more you have to do. (Get to do!) What you learn,\nand what that will lead to, is your own business, your own discovery, your own musical\nvision, your own musical direction, your own musical individuality. Ten different guitar-\nists who work with this material for at least one and hopefully two years, will come up\nwith ten totally different musical realities! (I\u2019ve already seen it happen, in that a very\nsmall number of my students over the last 4 or 5 years have been working with \u201cjust a bit\u201d\nof all this material for a while. Each and every one of them has come up with something\ndifferent!)\n\nThe main thing for me, is the \u201cdiscovery\u201d part of it. I was really lucky. I discovered\nit by myself. (In retrospect, given all my musical education, great teachers, and great mu-\nsical influences, I had a tremendous amount of help and preparative skill-training!) I don\u2019t\nwant to take away those experiences from any one guitarist (or all guitarists) by trying to\nexplain too much.\n\n\n\u201cNAME THAT CHORD\u201d\n\nVolume I consists of triads, 7* chords, triad over bass note I and triad over bass\nnote II (TBN I, TBN I). Triads are 3-part chords. The other 3 voicings are 4-part chords.\nTriads have simple, standardized names. No problem. 7* chords also have fairly simple\nand standardized names. Also, not a problem. However, TBN J and TBN II chords have\nmore complex and much less standardized names. The problem involves the fact that\nthe name is only accurate 25% of the time, (when the bass note is actually the lowest\nnote of the voicing.) The other 75% of the time (the other 3 inversions), the so-called\nbass-note is in the soprano (top), alto (2 from the top), or tenor (3\" from the top) voice.\nConsequently, the names for TBN I and TBN II structures are less than completely\nsatisfactory. (One could certainly make a case for the argument that, for example, Am/D\ncould just as easily have been called Am add D or Am add 4.) The chords are what they\nare regardless of what they\u2019re called. As long as our approach to the naming process is\nconsistent (and I believe it is), I think we'll be O.K. We'll just have to proceed as best we\ncan. (Besides, the chords couldn\u2019t care less what we call them!)\n\nQ: What is the difference between TBN I and TBN II?\n\nA: You could think of it this way: Even the 7* chords could be thought of (or named) as\nsome kind of triad over bass note. For example, Cmaj7 could be viewed as Em/C or\neven C/B in terms of arriving at the 4 different notes in the structure. However, as was\nmentioned earlier, since the 7* chords terminology is more consistent and standardized,\nwe'll go with that. We could think of TBN I and TBN II as being built up in thirds from\nthe 7* chord. For example, where Em/C is Cmaj7; moving the triad (Em) up a diatonic\nthird produces G/C, or TBN I. Moving the triad up a third again produces B\u00b0/C, or\nTBN I. Another way to think of it: In TBN I, the relationship of the triad to the bass note\nis a diatonic 5\"; in TBN IL, , the relationship of the triad to the bass note is a diatonic 7*.\nI hope that clarifies it for you.\n\nQ: What are cycles? Why use them?\n\nA: Cycles are ways of moving through a scale. Cycles can be melodic or harmonic. We\u2019re\ndealing with the harmonic aspect. Since we're exploring 3 different 7-note scales, (Major,\nMelodic Minor, Harmonic Minor) it turns out that there are 6 diatonic cycles. For pur-\nposes of consistency, I\u2019ve named all 6 cycles by ascending diatonic intervals.\n\n\u00b0 Cycle 2\u2014ascending a diatonic 24, (i.e, CM7, Dm/7, Em/7, etc.)\n* Cycle 4\u2014ascending a diatonic 4\u201c, (i.e, CM7, FM7, Bm7(b5), etc.)\n* Cycle 6\u2014ascending a diatonic 6\", (Le., CM7, Am7, FM/7, etc.)\nor descending a diatonic 3\", (easier)\n* Cycle 7\u2014ascending a diatonic 7*, (i.e, CM7, Bm7(b5), Am/7, etc.)\nor descending a diatonic 2\", (easier)\n\u00a2 Cycle 5\u2014ascending a diatonic 5*, (i.e, CM7, G7, Dm/7, etc.)\nor descending a diatonic 4\", (easier)\nCycle 3\u2014ascending a diatonic 3%, (i.e, CM7, Em7, G7, etc.)\n\no\nION IN NNN NN\n\noN\n\na\u201c,\n\nNNN NNN\n\nON NNN\n\n\nCycles 2 and 7 are the same in opposite directions\nCycles 4 and 5 are the same in opposite directions\nCycles 6 and 3 are the same in opposite directions\n\n*Note that what most of the musical world calls cycle 5, we will be calling cycle 4. (Sorry about that!)\n\nThe reason we use the cycles is because they cover all diatonic possibilities. There\nare no other ways of moving diatonically (within a 7-note scale) that are not included in\nthe 6 cycles using the same harmonic structure.\n\nAll of this may seem confusing just in the reading of it. But, I\u2019m sure that once you\nbegin to work with the material, it will gradually become very clear. (You could think of it\nthis way: using the same voicing type, if you voice-lead through all 6 cycles, you will have\nmoved from every chord to every other chord.)\n\nQ: Why do cycles 2 and 7 look so different from what you'd expect to see?\n\nA: That's a very good question. Technically speaking, we are featuring disjunct voice-\nleading with the triads for cycles 2 and 7. The most obvious way would have been:\n\nTriads: C major C Dm Em E G Am Be\nCycle: 2: E-\"F-\" G-\"A\"B\u2019\u201dC-\"D\nG-\"A-* BC-\"D_-YE-\" F\nCD\u201d E-VF_vG_-\"A_-\"B\n\nThe intervallic voice-leading would look like this:\n\n3 \u2014\u201d 2nd\n5 \u2014\u201d Ind\n1 -\u00a5 2nd\n\nThe functional voice-leading would look like this:\n\n2\nCs al)\n\nI deliberately didn\u2019t include this because it\u2019s been presented in other books before. For\nsomeone just starting with all of this, I\u2019d suggest that both approaches are very valuable.\n\nYW\n\n\nA similar thing happens with the 4-part chords through cycles 2 and 7:\n\n7 chords: CM7 Dm7Z EmZ FMZ GZ Am7 Bm7b5\nCycle: 2 G-\"A-\"B C-\"D-\"E -\"F\nEF -7G \u201cA-YB-\"C \"D\nBvVC 7D VYE-VF \"GUA\nC vrD VE vwF_vG_-vVA vB\n\nThe intervallic voice-leading would look like this:\n\n5 \u20147 Ind\n3 \u2014\u201d Ind\n7 \u2014* Ind\n1 -* Qnd\n\nThe functional voice-leading would look like this:\n\n1\nC7\n\u00a9\nThis would be the most obvious voice-leading if a guitarist were given just the chord\n\nsymbols. It\u2019s certainly an important first step, but, like the triads, this type of voice-lead-\ning has already been demonstrated in other books.\n\nm= NEW UI\n\nVAWA\n\n\nQ: Why do you include 4-way close voicings?\n\nA: Because some of them are playable as chords. It\u2019s also interesting to use open strings\nwith other fretted notes. But the main reason they\u2019re included is because all of them are\nplayable as arpeggios. This is also true with some other seemingly impossible-to-play\nvoicings. (Some of the Drop 2 & 3, for example.) If you can\u2019t play a voicing as a chord, you\ncan always play the notes in some kind of arpeggio.\n\nQ: Why Major scale, Melodic Minor scale, Harmonic Minor scale?\n\nA: If music was painting, the Major, Melodic Minor, Harmonic Minor scales would be the\nequivalent of the colors red, yellow and blue. These three 7-note scales have been around\nfor thousands of years. You'll come to see that they contain incredible harmonic variety\nand richness.\n\nQ: Are there any other ways of voice-leading through the cycles?\n\nA: Absolutely! What we're doing in Vol. I and II is just simple common-tone voice-lead-\ning. Other ways of voice-leading will be discussed in a future volume.\n\nQ: What is M.S.R.P.?\n\nA:M.S.R.P. stands for Melodic Strand Replication Procedure. It\u2019s aname I came up with to\ndescribe some very interesting things that showed up while I was figuring out all these\nvoice-leadings. Again, I\u2019m deliberately not going to go into much detail about all of this so\nthat each person can \u201cre-discover\u201d it just like I did.\n\nOn each page you'll see Intervallic Voice-leading, Functional Voice-leading, (of which\nthere are two different depictions) and M.S.R_P. at the bottom. All of them are essentially\nthe same thing. Just different ways of looking at it. However, it does get really interesting\nwhen you see that all of these chords and voicings are also melodies. (Actually, in most\ncases, a single melody!) So this is the musical version of \u201cWhat came first; the chicken or\nthe egg?\u201d i.e, \u201cWhat came first; melody or harmony?\u201d (to which I would reply to both\nquestions: \u201cBoth arose simultaneously.\u201d) M.S.R.P. is like melodic D.N.A. that makes up\nharmony. Good luck!!\n\nQ: You mentioned earlier that you would be giving tentative guidelines concerning the left\n(fingering) hand. What are they?\n\nA: Ilied....sort of!\n\nActually, that\u2019s very difficult to do, given all the possible combinations of different hand\nand finger sizes; long, medium and short scale necks; high, medium and low string\ntensions; high, medium and low string actions, etc., etc., etc.\n\nHowever, the main thing is to follow the advice given in the DIS@LAIMER/WARNING/\nCAUTIONI\u00ae! Section. (Go back and read it again, right now!) If everyone does that we'll\nall be just fine!\n\n\nIn addition, here are some other points to consider:\n\u00b0 Be cautious of any stretches greater than 4 frets beneath the V* position.\n\u00a2 Remember that arpeggiating the notes is always an option.\n* You can, at any time, transpose the voicing up one octave, thus making the\nstretch considerably less.\n\nKeep in mind that voicings which seem \u201ctoo much of a stretch\u201d look very\ndifferent in other keys.\n\nQ: Isn\u2019t it true that musicians who play other harmonic instruments besides guitar could learn a\nlot by exploring this material?\n\nA: Yes. But please don\u2019t tell them!\n\nQ: Wouldn't composers and arrangers benefit as well, especially considering that they don\u2019t have to\ndeal with the limitations of the guitar?\n\nA: Shhhhi!!!\n\nQ: When can I start to move \u201call this stuff\u201d to other keys? And what key should I choose?\n\nA: Same answer to both questions: You don\u2019t move it. Itmoves by itself. Just watch! (Don\u2019t\nbe in a hurry\u2014\u2014) When it does move, don\u2019t assume that you shouldn\u2019t continue with \u201call\nthis stuff\u201d in C Major, C Melodic Minor and C Harmonic Minor. (How much of this mate-\nrial do you really think you have explored, let alone learned?)\n\nQ: What is the best way to learn all this stuff?\n\nA: There is no best way\n\nQ: O.K. Ah....Is there a worst way?\n\nA: Yes! By trying to find the best way.\n\nQ: O.K. Al... ceecccaes Do you have any suggestions?\n\nA: Yes. Don\u2019t try to learn it. In fact, try not to learn it!\n\nQ: I don\u2019t think I understand that.\n\nA: If you don\u2019t try to learn it, if you try not to learn it, you completely eliminate any self-\nimposed (or \u201cother-imposed\u201d) pressure. You just have the experiences of going through\n\nthe material. That will be enough.\n\n10\n\n\nThere are so many aspects and ways in which a guitarist can learn \u201call this stuff,\u201d\nthat you don\u2019t want to short-change yourself by \u201clearning\u201d it too fast. Just consider some\nthings about how a guitarist learns:\n\n\u00a9 Tactile (touch)\n\nShape (configuration-visual)\nMovement (kinesthetic)\nRepetition (memory)\n\n\u00a2 Simultaneous melodies (gulp!)\n\u00a9 Understanding (analysis-theoretical)\n\u00a2 Sonic (sound)\n\n\u00a2 Emotional (feel)\n\n\u00a9 Gestalt (context-function)\n\n\u00a9 Other\n\n\u00a2 Unknown\n\n* Etc., etc., etc.\n\nIt\u2019s certainly possible that a guitarist could learn some of this material very quickly.\n{I've already seen this happen with a few of my students.) The obvious plus-side is that\nthey can repeat a cycle with a voicing through a scale from memory. And that\u2019s O.K. But\nthe less obvious minus-side is that they can\u2019t really use any of that material in a musical\ncontext. They didn\u2019t give themselves the chance to \u201clearn all this stuff\u201d in many different\nways at many different levels.\n\nDon\u2019t be in a hurry. Take your time. Find your own pace. Relax into your own\nlearning process, and let the material do to you (and with you) what it (and you) need. It\nand you both need time to grow.\n\nI realize that what follows may sound a little weird, or a bit of a stretch (pun in-\ntended), but I'll say it anyway have faith that the structure, consistency, scope and\napproach to this material (\u201call this stuff\u201d) has an intelligence all it\u2019s own. It\u2019s almost as if it\nwere alive. If you work with it, it will \u201cspeak\u201d to you. It will tell you things. It will present\nyou with possibilities that you had NEVER considered before. It will give you incredible\n\u201cbits and pieces\u201d that you will feel compelled to extend and complete.\n\nLet me leave it at that. Check it out!\n\n\u2014Mick Gaadrick Basten, July 2000\n\n11\n\n\n(12 12\n\nTriads\nClose & Spread\nAll Cycles\nC Major\n\n14 14\n\nChord Family: Triad\nCycle: 2\nS